\DOC LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across one inlined
modular-multiplication subroutine, replacing the current symbolic state with a
later one and adding an assumption characterising the computed product modulo
{p_521}.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used in locating the call site,
which affects the instruction-count/state advance; the second is a
{string list} naming the output buffer and the two input buffers of the
multiplication, which determines the names appearing in the new result
assumption. The conclusion retains the same {eventually} postcondition, but its
final state variable is replaced by a fresh, later state variable. Every
assumption mentioning the old state — the program-counter value,
{bytes_loaded}, register reads, memory-content reads, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} relation — is discharged and re-added for the new state:
{MAYCHANGE} is extended with the newly written memory region, and the
register/memory reads are updated to the new state, with the destination and
source pointer registers now holding the addresses given by the name arguments.
A new assumption {t < p_521 /\ u < p_521 ==> v = (t * u) MOD p_521} relates the
named output to the product of the named inputs. Assumptions that do not refer
to the state, such as nonoverlapping conditions and earlier arithmetic
characterisations, are kept, though their order may change.
{
          A ?- t
   ========================  LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC n ["v"; "t"; "u"]
          A' ?- t'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions restated for
the new state plus the new product assumption, and {t'} is {t} with the state
variable advanced.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces this is the only outcome, always on goals of this
{eventually}-with-machine-state shape.

\ENDDOC
